  <title>Your recovery key and passphrase</title>
  <desc>Why you need the recovery key and how to use your backups after a reinstall.</desc>

  <p>Your backups are encrypted. Delta keeps their passphrase in your desktop keyring and uses it by itself, so you never type it.</p>

  <note style="warning">
    <title>Keep the recovery key away from this computer</title>
    <p>If you lose access to your keyring (a new computer, a reinstall, a damaged profile), the passphrase goes with it.
    The recovery key the assistant gave you is then the only way to read your backups. A key kept only on the disk that fails
    protects nothing: print it, or keep it in a password manager or on a USB drive somewhere else.</p>
  </note>

  <steps>
    <title>Use your backups after a reinstall</title>
    <item><p>Install Delta and open it.</p></item>
    <item><p>In the assistant, choose the folder or drive that holds your backups.</p></item>
    <item><p>Delta finds the existing repository: enter the recovery key (or your passphrase) as
    <gui>Repository passphrase</gui> and choose <gui style="button">Use this repository</gui>. Nothing in it is erased.</p></item>
  </steps>

  <p>Delta never stores the passphrase in a plain file, in the settings or on a command line. It never sends your backups anywhere,
  and it never overwrites your files when you restore.</p>
